# Sign leaves through the one role, and nothing else.
#
# DELIBERATELY NARROWER THAN AWS, whose copy of this file grants
# create/read/update/delete/list on `pki_private_issuer/*`. That covers the
# issuer configuration, the CA endpoints, every role and the tidy/revoke APIs --
# so a leaked secret_id could rotate the issuer, or add a role allowing any
# domain, rather than only mint a certificate for the domain this role permits.
#
# cert-manager's Vault issuer needs exactly two things at runtime:
#   - POST auth/approle/login  -- unauthenticated by definition, needs no policy
#   - POST <mount>/sign/<role> -- this rule
# Its readiness probe hits sys/health, which is unauthenticated by default.
#
# TEMPLATED, unlike the AWS copy, and that is the point of the divergence: the
# path names the role, the role is what constrains the domain, and a `sign/*`
# wildcard would re-admit any role added later. Deriving both halves from the
# resources themselves (policies.tf) means renaming the mount or the role cannot
# silently leave this policy pointing at a path that no longer exists -- which
# fails open in the sense that matters: cert-manager gets a 403 and certificates
# stop renewing, with nothing here to explain why.
path "${mount}/sign/${role}" {
  capabilities = ["create", "update"]
}
